    <tr><td><strong>Painting / Finish Wait</strong></td><td>None — doors finished in factory</td><td>5–10 additional days for painter, drying time</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Dust &amp; Disruption</strong></td><td>Minimal — no sawing or sanding on site</td><td>Significant — sawdust, paint fumes, daily workers in kitchen</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Total Site-to-Handover</strong></td><td>3–5 weeks (including manufacturing)</td><td>3–6 weeks (all on site)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Post-Install Adjustment</strong></td><td>Hardware adjustment during or immediately after install</td><td>Snags may take days to weeks; re-calling the carpenter adds time</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h2>Customisation — Who Offers More Flexibility?</h2>
<p>The assumption that carpenter kitchens are more customisable is based on a legitimate observation: a carpenter can build whatever shape is asked for, on site, without catalogue constraints. In practice, the reality is more nuanced — and for most kitchens, the modular system offers equal or greater flexibility.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Modular Kitchen — Customisation Strengths</span><div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><h3>Where Modular Wins</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Any wall length — modules cut to exact dimension</li>
  <li>Non-standard ceiling heights accommodated with filler panels</li>
  <li>Unlimited finish combinations: colour, material, texture, handle style</li>
  <li>Interior hardware options are far wider than carpenter builds</li>
  <li>Mix of open shelves, glass shutters, solid doors in one design</li>
  <li>3D design approval ensures what you approve is what you receive</li>
  <li>Island and peninsula configurations with structural support</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Carpenter Kitchen — Customisation Strengths</span><h3>Where Carpenter Wins</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Highly irregular spaces — curved walls, triangular niches</li>
  <li>Very low-budget requirements where only basic fixed shelves are needed</li>
  <li>Immediate availability without manufacturing lead time</li>
  <li>Small repairs or additions to existing carpenter joinery</li>
  <li>Very simple requirements with no hardware beyond basic hinges</li>
  <li>Remote locations where modular delivery is impractical</li>
</ul></div></div>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Enteriors Insight</span>The argument that carpenters are more customisable collapses when examined closely. "Customisable" means you can ask for any shape — but without a 3D design process, there is no reliable way to verify that what you asked for is what you will receive. Modular kitchens begin with a documented design that is approved before manufacturing. The output matches the agreed design. With a carpenter kitchen, the first time you see the actual result is when the cabinet is already fixed to the wall.</div>
<h2>Maintenance — The 5-Year Reality</h2>
<p>Maintenance is the factor most homeowners underestimate when comparing kitchen options. The cumulative cost, effort, and disruption of maintaining a carpenter kitchen over 5–10 years frequently exceeds the initial saving. Here is what to expect from each option.</p>
<p>Modular — Hinge Adjustment</p>
<p>Concealed hinges may require a one-time adjustment 6–12 months after installation as the carcass settles. This is a 10-minute job with a screwdriver. No specialist required.</p>
<p>Modular — Drawer Runner Service</p>
<p>Full-extension runners benefit from occasional lubrication every 2–3 years. A silicone-based spray lubricant applied to the runner track is all that is required.</p>
<p>Carpenter — Edge Tape Replacement</p>
<p>Iron-on edge tape lifts at corners and near water sources within 1–3 years. Re-gluing delays the issue; replacement requires removing the door, stripping the edge, and re-applying — typically ₹500–1500 per door.</p>
<p>Carpenter — Hinge Replacement</p>
<p>Local-brand hinges begin to fail — springs weaken, soft-close stops working, and doors sag — within 2–4 years. Full hinge replacement across a kitchen with 20+ doors costs ₹4,000–15,000 including labour.</p>
<p>Carpenter — Repainting</p>
<p>Site-applied duco or PU paint in a kitchen environment typically shows yellowing, grease absorption, and micro-cracking at joints within 4–6 years. Full kitchen repaint costs ₹15,000–40,000 and requires the kitchen to be unusable for 3–5 days.</p>
<p>Carpenter — Swollen Board Replacement</p>
<p>Boards near the sink or dishwasher that were not properly edge-sealed will swell and delaminate. Replacing a base cabinet typically costs ₹8,000–20,000 including materials and labour, plus the disruption of removing plumbing connections.</p>
